WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.592
Now let's talk about one of the most important objectives during a game: revealing

2
00:00:04.612 --> 00:00:05.200
hidden cards.

3
00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:07.567
At the beginning, most tableau cards are face-down.

4
00:00:07.633 --> 00:00:09.933
You cannot see what they are, and you cannot move them.

5
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.233
The only way to access them is to move the face-up cards that are covering them.

6
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:14.333
Let's look at an example.

7
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.433
Imagine one column has a black nine covering a hidden card.

8
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.267
You find a red ten in another column.

9
00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:21.467
You can move the black nine onto the red ten.

10
00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:23.367
That exposes the hidden card underneath.

11
00:00:23.433 --> 00:00:26.433
And that is a meaningful move, because you have gained new information.

12
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.067
The newly revealed card might be exactly what you need.

13
00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:31.267
It could be a King that lets you use an empty column.

14
00:00:31.333 --> 00:00:33.433
It could be an Ace that belongs in a foundation.

15
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.767
Or it could be a card that unlocks another sequence.

16
00:00:35.833 --> 00:00:38.433
But revealing a card is not automatically the best move.

17
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.367
You should consider what you're giving up to reveal it.

18
00:00:40.433 --> 00:00:40.988
For example,

19
00:00:41.008 --> 00:00:44.867
if moving a card exposes a hidden card but blocks an important sequence somewhere

20
00:00:44.887 --> 00:00:46.467
else, you may want to reconsider.

21
00:00:46.533 --> 00:00:50.049
A good general habit is to look for moves that reveal face-down cards while

22
00:00:50.069 --> 00:00:51.767
preserving useful options elsewhere.

23
00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:55.000
Another important point is that not all columns are equally valuable.

24
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:58.800
A column with several hidden cards may offer more opportunities to discover useful

25
00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:01.200
cards than a column that is already mostly revealed.

26
00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:03.104
So, when you have multiple legal moves,

27
00:01:03.124 --> 00:01:06.267
pay attention to which move gives you the most useful information.

28
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:09.705
This is one of the first ways you move from simply playing Solitaire to actually

29
00:01:09.725 --> 00:01:10.700
thinking strategically.

30
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:13.867
Next, let's look at the foundations.

31
00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:18.146
The objective of a standard Klondike Solitaire game is to move all fifty-two cards

32
00:01:18.166 --> 00:01:19.733
into the four foundation piles.

33
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.900
Each foundation corresponds to one suit.

34
00:01:21.967 --> 00:01:23.267
You always start with an Ace.

35
00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:24.700
Then you build upward in order.

36
00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:28.667
Two, three, four, five, and so on, all the way to King.

37
00:01:28.733 --> 00:01:29.800
You cannot skip a rank.

38
00:01:29.867 --> 00:01:32.033
And you cannot mix suits within a foundation.

39
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.504
For example, the hearts foundation must contain the Ace of hearts,

40
00:01:35.524 --> 00:01:39.000
followed by the two of hearts, then the three of hearts, and so on.

41
00:01:39.067 --> 00:01:41.800
The same applies to diamonds, clubs, and spades.

42
00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:43.431
Whenever an Ace becomes available,

43
00:01:43.451 --> 00:01:45.967
it's usually a good idea to move it to its foundation.

44
00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:48.667
The same is often true for a two that can legally follow it.

45
00:01:48.733 --> 00:01:50.267
But be careful with higher cards.

46
00:01:50.333 --> 00:01:52.345
Moving a card to a foundation can be useful,

47
00:01:52.365 --> 00:01:55.333
but it can also remove a card that you might need in the tableau.

48
00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.495
Imagine you have a red six on a foundation,

49
00:01:57.515 --> 00:02:00.467
and later realize you need that six to receive a black five.

50
00:02:00.533 --> 00:02:03.928
You may be able to take the six back from the foundation and return it to the

51
00:02:03.948 --> 00:02:05.633
tableau, as long as the move is legal.

52
00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.367
This is called a foundation takeback.

53
00:02:07.433 --> 00:02:09.200
And yes, it is allowed in GRYND.

54
00:02:09.267 --> 00:02:11.700
So don't think of foundation moves as permanently locked.

55
00:02:11.767 --> 00:02:15.267
You can sometimes reverse them when doing so helps you reorganize the board.

56
00:02:15.333 --> 00:02:17.700
The important thing is to understand why you're moving a card.

57
00:02:17.767 --> 00:02:20.674
Foundations are where you're ultimately trying to send every card,

58
00:02:20.694 --> 00:02:23.633
but the tableau is where you create the opportunities to get there.

59
00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.967
Good Solitaire players balance those two areas.

60
00:02:27.367 --> 00:02:29.133
Now let's look at the stock and waste piles.

61
00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:29.599
Remember,

62
00:02:29.619 --> 00:02:33.200
the stock contains the twenty-four cards that weren't dealt into the tableau.

63
00:02:33.267 --> 00:02:36.800
When you draw from the stock, one card is revealed and placed in the waste.

64
00:02:36.867 --> 00:02:38.400
GRYND uses a single-card draw.

65
00:02:38.467 --> 00:02:40.100
That means you see one card at a time.

66
00:02:40.167 --> 00:02:42.267
The top card of the waste is available to play.

67
00:02:42.333 --> 00:02:46.057
You can move it to a valid tableau position or to a foundation if the move follows

68
00:02:46.077 --> 00:02:46.533
the rules.

69
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:50.066
Once you draw another card, the previous waste card is no longer the top card,

70
00:02:50.086 --> 00:02:52.500
so you need to work with the card currently available.

71
00:02:52.567 --> 00:02:54.400
This means the order of your draws matters.

72
00:02:54.467 --> 00:02:55.666
You might see a useful card,

73
00:02:55.686 --> 00:02:58.867
but you may need to move another card before it becomes accessible again.

74
00:02:58.933 --> 00:02:59.521
And sometimes,

75
00:02:59.541 --> 00:03:03.100
drawing through the stock helps you discover a move that changes the entire board.

76
00:03:03.167 --> 00:03:04.867
What happens when the stock becomes empty?

77
00:03:04.933 --> 00:03:05.733
You can redeal.

78
00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:07.631
The waste is reversed back into the stock,

79
00:03:07.651 --> 00:03:09.633
allowing you to draw through the cards again.

80
00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.567
In GRYND, redeals are unlimited.

81
00:03:11.633 --> 00:03:13.833
So you are not limited to one pass through the stock.

82
00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.900
You can continue cycling through the cards as you search for useful moves.

83
00:03:16.967 --> 00:03:20.433
However, unlimited redeals do not mean you should draw without thinking.

84
00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:23.500
Pay attention to which cards become available, what you can move,

85
00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:26.400
and whether a tableau move could reveal something more useful.

86
00:03:26.467 --> 00:03:30.320
A common beginner mistake is to keep drawing cards automatically without looking at

87
00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:30.867
the tableau.

88
00:03:30.933 --> 00:03:31.867
Try to avoid that.

89
00:03:31.933 --> 00:03:34.367
Before drawing again, take a moment to scan the board.

90
00:03:34.433 --> 00:03:36.333
You might already have a useful move available.

91
00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.700
And if you don't, the stock is there to help you explore your remaining options.

92
00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.627
Now that we understand the rules,

93
00:03:42.647 --> 00:03:45.367
let's combine them into an actual decision-making process.

94
00:03:45.433 --> 00:03:47.033
Imagine you have just started a new game.

95
00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.567
You see seven tableau columns, four foundations, and a stock pile.

96
00:03:50.633 --> 00:03:51.767
What should you look for first?

97
00:03:51.833 --> 00:03:53.167
Start by scanning the tableau.

98
00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:54.033
Look for Aces.

99
00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.700
If an Ace is available, you can move it to its foundation.

100
00:03:56.767 --> 00:03:59.267
Then look for any immediately legal tableau moves.

101
00:03:59.333 --> 00:04:02.800
Remember the two conditions: descending rank and alternating colors.

102
00:04:02.867 --> 00:04:05.633
Next, look for opportunities to reveal hidden cards.

103
00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.302
If you can move a card and uncover another card underneath,

104
00:04:08.322 --> 00:04:09.700
that may give you more options.

105
00:04:09.767 --> 00:04:11.067
Now look at the empty columns.

106
00:04:11.133 --> 00:04:14.033
If one exists, consider whether you have a King that can use it.

107
00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.659
But don't move a King automatically.

108
00:04:15.679 --> 00:04:18.267
Think about which King creates the most useful arrangement.

109
00:04:18.333 --> 00:04:19.767
After that, look at the waste.

110
00:04:19.833 --> 00:04:20.933
Is its top card playable?

111
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.133
Can it go to a foundation?

112
00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.000
Can it help create a sequence in the tableau?

113
00:04:24.067 --> 00:04:27.443
Only after checking those possibilities should you consider drawing another card

114
00:04:27.463 --> 00:04:28.100
from the stock.
